@module flutter-m15 | Architecture
@summary Structure apps with MVC, MVVM, Clean Architecture, repositories, DI and SOLID.

@lesson flutter-m15-l01 | MVC | 8
## Learning objectives
- Understand Model-View-Controller

## MVC
MVC separates data (Model), UI (View) and input handling (Controller). In Flutter, widgets are the View; controllers or state objects handle actions.

```dart
class CounterModel { int value = 0; }
class CounterController {
  CounterController(this.model);
  final CounterModel model;
  void increment() => model.value++;
}
```

## Summary
- Separate data, UI and actions.

@lesson flutter-m15-l02 | MVVM | 8
## Learning objectives
- Use ViewModels to expose UI state

## MVVM
In MVVM the View observes a ViewModel that exposes ready-to-display state and commands; the ViewModel talks to repositories. The Flutter team's architecture guide recommends this pattern.

```dart
import 'package:flutter/foundation.dart';

class LessonsViewModel extends ChangeNotifier {
  List<String> lessons = [];
  bool loading = false;
  Future<void> load(Future<List<String>> Function() repo) async {
    loading = true; notifyListeners();
    lessons = await repo();
    loading = false; notifyListeners();
  }
}
```

## Summary
- Views observe ViewModels; ViewModels call repositories.

@lesson flutter-m15-l03 | Clean Architecture | 8
## Learning objectives
- Separate presentation, domain and data layers

## Clean Architecture
Clean Architecture splits code into presentation (UI, ViewModels), domain (entities, use cases, repository interfaces) and data (API/DB implementations). Dependencies point inward to the domain.

```dart
// domain
abstract interface class ProgressRepository { Future<Set<String>> completed(); }
class GetNextLesson {
  GetNextLesson(this.repo);
  final ProgressRepository repo;
  Future<String> call(List<String> order) async {
    final done = await repo.completed();
    return order.firstWhere((id) => !done.contains(id), orElse: () => order.last);
  }
}
```

## Summary
- Domain has no Flutter or database imports.

@lesson flutter-m15-l04 | Repository pattern | 8
## Learning objectives
- Hide data sources behind repositories

## Repository pattern
A repository offers domain-friendly methods and decides whether data comes from cache, database or network.

```dart
abstract interface class WeatherRepository { Future<double> temperature(String city); }

class CachedWeatherRepository implements WeatherRepository {
  CachedWeatherRepository(this.remote);
  final Future<double> Function(String) remote;
  final _cache = <String, double>{};
  @override
  Future<double> temperature(String city) async => _cache[city] ??= await remote(city);
}
```

## Summary
- UI never talks to APIs directly.

@lesson flutter-m15-l05 | Dependency injection | 8
## Learning objectives
- Provide dependencies from outside

## Dependency injection
Pass dependencies into constructors instead of creating them inside classes. Provide them with Provider/Riverpod or a service locator like get_it, and swap fakes in tests.

```dart
class AuthService { AuthService(this.api); final Object api; }
final auth = AuthService(Object()); // composed at app start
```

## Summary
- Constructor injection makes code testable.

@lesson flutter-m15-l06 | SOLID principles | 8
## Learning objectives
- Apply SOLID to Dart code

## SOLID principles
Single responsibility, Open/closed, Liskov substitution, Interface segregation, Dependency inversion. They lead to small classes that depend on abstractions.

```dart
abstract interface class Logger { void log(String m); }
class ConsoleLogger implements Logger { @override void log(String m) => print(m); }
class Checkout { Checkout(this.logger); final Logger logger; }
```

## Summary
- Depend on abstractions, keep classes focused.

@lesson flutter-m15-l07 | Feature-based architecture | 8
## Learning objectives
- Organise code by feature

## Feature-based architecture
Group code by feature (auth, lessons, profile), each with its own data/domain/presentation folders, plus a shared core.

```dart
// lib/features/lessons/{data,domain,presentation}/
// lib/core/{theme,network,utils}/
void main() {}
```

## Summary
- Feature folders scale better than type folders.

@lesson flutter-m15-l08 | Error handling architecture | 8
## Learning objectives
- Handle errors consistently across layers

## Error handling architecture
Data layers catch exceptions and convert them into typed failures (sealed classes or Result types); ViewModels map failures to UI states; a global handler reports unexpected errors.

```dart
sealed class Failure { const Failure(this.message); final String message; }
class NetworkFailure extends Failure { const NetworkFailure() : super('Check your connection'); }
class ServerFailure extends Failure { const ServerFailure() : super('Something went wrong'); }
```

## Summary
- Typed failures make UI error states exhaustive.

@lesson flutter-m15-l09 | Maintainable project organization | 8
## Learning objectives
- Keep a growing codebase healthy

## Maintainable project organization
Use lints, consistent naming, small files, documented public APIs, CI checks and code review. Refactor continuously instead of in big rewrites.

```dart
// analysis_options.yaml
// include: package:flutter_lints/flutter.yaml
void main() {}
```

## Summary
- Automate quality checks in CI.
